#!/usr/bin/python3
"""The appliance's web setup, on the appliance's side (suveryn-tracker#37, part 5). The screens are
suveryn-core's chat-ui (setup.html); this answers them, keeps the answers and runs provisioning.

    suveryn-setup prepare     root, before serve: what the screens show, the setup certificate, nginx
    suveryn-setup serve       the setup API on 127.0.0.1:8090, as the unprivileged user suveryn-setup
    suveryn-setup provision   root: provisioning with the saved answers, then closing setup
    suveryn-setup watch       root, without systemd (a test machine): runs provision when it is asked for

Three parts, so that nothing reachable from the network runs as root:

- **prepare** (`ExecStartPre=+` in suveryn-setup.service) writes `facts.json` (the name, the
  language chosen at installation, the appliance's identity, the installed models), makes the
  appliance's own certificate for `<name>.local` and its address (the local CA's, which provisioning
  keeps when the office chooses it), and gives nginx the setup site.
- **serve** answers the screens through nginx (`/setup/v1/`, from private addresses only). It checks
  every answer as the screens do, saves it in its own folder (`answers/`, 0700) and asks for
  provisioning by writing `answers/start`. It can't do anything else: no root, no capabilities, no
  write access outside that folder (suveryn-setup.service).
- **provision** (suveryn-setup-provision.service, started by a path unit when `start` appears)
  reads the answers back as untrusted input, checks them again, runs
  `suveryn-provision first-boot` with them and reports progress in `progress.json`. Once the
  browser has shown Complete it closes setup: the office's own certificate (if chosen) goes in,
  the setup locations leave nginx, `/etc/suveryn/setup-complete` is written, the answers' secrets
  are deleted and these units are disabled.

The API is the contract at the top of suveryn-core's packages/chat-ui/src/setup/api.ts. Refusals
are 422 with `{"errors": {field: code}}` or `{"error": code}`; the codes are shown in the office's
language (text.ts, `errors`). Python 3 standard library, openssl, ip and lsblk only.
"""

